---
import GlossaryEntry from '../../layouts/GlossaryEntry.astro';
---
<GlossaryEntry
  category="Arduino IDE"
  title="LittleFS Upload Plugin"
  subtitle="How to install and use the ESP32 LittleFS filesystem uploader"
  backTo="/guides/max98357a-littlefs#uploading"
  backLabel="Back"
  highlights={[
    'The plugin adds a filesystem upload option to Arduino IDE',
    'Files in your sketch data/ folder get uploaded to the ESP32',
    'Extract the .vsix (it is a zip) and put the folder into ~/.arduinoIDE/plugins/',
    'Upload the filesystem separately from the sketch - they are independent',
  ]}
>

  <Fragment slot="desc">
    <p>Arduino IDE does not upload the <code>data/</code> folder by default. The LittleFS plugin adds that ability - it packages everything in the folder into a filesystem image and flashes it to the ESP32's filesystem partition.</p>

    <h2>Arduino IDE 2.x - install the plugin</h2>

    <h3>Step 1 - Download the plugin</h3>
    <p>Go to the <a href="https://github.com/earlephilhower/arduino-littlefs-upload/releases" class="term-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arduino-littlefs-upload releases page &rarr;</a> and download the latest <code>.vsix</code> file.</p>
    <figure class="screenshot-wrap">
      <img src="/images/little1.png" alt="GitHub releases page showing the arduino-littlefs-upload .vsix file to download" />
      <figcaption>Download the .vsix from the Assets section</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Step 2 - Extract and install</h3>
    <p>The <code>.vsix</code> is a zip file. Extract it - you will get a folder. That folder goes into <code>~/.arduinoIDE/plugins/</code>.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Close Arduino IDE completely</li>
      <li>Create the plugins folder:</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="cmd-label">Copy and paste into terminal</p>
    <div class="code-wrap">
      <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.arduinoIDE/plugins</code></pre>
      <button class="copy-btn">Copy</button>
    </div>
    <ol start="3">
      <li>Right-click the <code>.vsix</code> file and extract it - or rename it to <code>.zip</code> and extract</li>
      <li>Move the extracted folder into <code>~/.arduinoIDE/plugins/</code></li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="screenshot-wrap">
      <img src="/images/little2.png" alt="File manager showing ~/.arduinoIDE/ with the plugins folder" />
      <figcaption>The plugins folder inside ~/.arduinoIDE/</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="screenshot-wrap">
      <img src="/images/little3.png" alt="File manager showing the extracted plugin folder inside ~/.arduinoIDE/plugins/" />
      <figcaption>Extracted plugin folder placed inside plugins/</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <ol start="5">
      <li>Restart Arduino IDE</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>The folder is hidden.</strong>
      <code>.arduinoIDE</code> starts with a dot, which makes it hidden by default on Linux. Press <strong>Ctrl+H</strong> in your file manager to show hidden folders.
    </div>

    <h3>Step 3 - Confirm it installed</h3>
    <p>Open the command palette (<strong>Ctrl+Shift+P</strong>) and type <strong>Upload LittleFS</strong>. If <strong>Upload LittleFS to Pico/ESP8266/ESP32</strong> appears, the plugin is ready.</p>


    <h2>How to upload the filesystem</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Make sure your sketch has a <code>data/</code> folder with your files in it</li>
      <li>Connect your ESP32 via USB</li>
      <li>Close the Serial Monitor if it is open - it locks the port</li>
      <li>Open the command palette: <strong>Ctrl+Shift+P</strong>, type and select <strong>Upload LittleFS</strong></li>
      <li>Wait for <strong>LittleFS upload finished</strong> in the output panel</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout callout-why">
      <strong>Sketch and filesystem are uploaded separately.</strong>
      Uploading the filesystem does not touch your sketch, and uploading the sketch does not touch the filesystem. Flash the sketch first via the normal Upload button, then upload the filesystem. After that, to swap clips out you only need to upload the filesystem again - no sketch reflash needed.
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</GlossaryEntry>

<style>
  pre {
    background: #1e1e2e;
    color: #cdd6f4;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
  }
  pre code {
    font-family: "Fira Code", "Cascadia Code", Consolas, monospace;
    background: none;
    padding: 0;
    color: inherit;
  }
  code {
    font-family: "Fira Code", "Cascadia Code", Consolas, monospace;
    background: #f0f0f0;
    padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-size: 0.875em;
    color: #c0392b;
  }
  .screenshot-wrap {
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .screenshot-wrap img {
    width: 100%;
    display: block;
  }
  .screenshot-wrap figcaption {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: #888;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem;
    background: #fafafa;
    border-top: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
  }

  .callout-why {
    background: #f5f0ff;
    border-left: 4px solid #c47a1e;
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .callout-why strong { display: block; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
</style>

<script is:inline>
  document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn').forEach(btn => {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const pre = btn.closest('.code-wrap').querySelector('pre');
      navigator.clipboard.writeText(pre.innerText).then(() => {
        btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
        btn.classList.add('copied');
        setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = 'Copy'; btn.classList.remove('copied'); }, 1500);
      });
    });
  });
</script>
